  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Melitta melittoides is a small, solitary bee species belonging to the Melittidae family, commonly found throughout New England. These bees are important native pollinators that specialize in foraging on specific plant families.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Body Size and Color",
      "description": "Small bee, typically 8-12 mm in length, with a dark body and distinctive reddish-brown or golden abdominal markings",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Facial Features",
      "description": "Broad face with relatively large compound eyes and a short mouthpart structure adapted for specific flowers",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Wing Pattern",
      "description": "Clear wings with a characteristic venation pattern typical of melittid bees, lacking the pigmentation seen in some other bee families",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Melitta melittoides inhabits open woodlands, meadows, and garden areas throughout New England where their preferred flowering plants are abundant.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike honeybees, Melitta melittoides is a solitary bee that nests individually in the ground, and females are oligolectic, meaning they collect pollen from only a few specific plant species rather than visiting a wide variety of flowers.",
